\begin_layout Title
Chapter 19: Censored Data, Sample Selection, and Attrition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
19.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In previous chapters, we assume that we can obtain a random sample from
 the population of interest.
 In this chapter, we turn to several missing data problems.
 It is critical, even more so than in the previous chapters, to distinguish
 between assumptions placed on the population model and assumptions about
 how the data were generated.
 Under random sampling, the itneresting issues concern the population model
 and assumptions we make about distributional features in the population.
 With nonrandom sampling, we must take particular care in stating assumptions
 about the population and separately stating assumptions on the sampling
 scheme.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We first study the general problem of 
\series bold
data censoring
\series default
.
 With data censoring we can still randomly sample 
\emph on
units
\emph default
 from the relevant population, but we face the problem that one or more
 of the variables is censored: we only observe the response over a certain
 range.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In addition to data censoring, we also treat the general problem of sample
 selection.
 With sample selection problems we may or may not be able to randomly sample
 units from the population.
 The case of 
\series bold
data truncation 
\series default
is a situation where we do not randomly draw units from the population;
 rather, we randomly sample from a subpopulation defined in terms of one
 or more of the observed variables.
 Naturally, the population parameters cannot always be identified with such
 sampling schemes, but they can be under suitable assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another sample selection problem is 
\series bold
incidental truncation
\series default
, where certain variables are observed only if other variables take on particula
r values.
 In such cases, we often can randomly sample units, but we have data missing
 on key varibles (and, unlike in the data censoring case, we have no information
 on the outcome, or even a range of possible outcomes, of the missing data).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
19.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Data Censoring
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Although the statistical models used for limited dependent variables and
 censored dependent variables are similar, their interpretaiton, as well
 as the way one views and reacts to violations of standard assumptions,
 is very different.
 Because the statistical tools for handling censored data are very similar
 to specifying and estimating models for limited dependent variables, our
 coverage of the estimation details can be terse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Our main focus on this chaper is on a very familiar model: the linear model
 from inroductory econometrics.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 denote the variable of interest and assume that it follows a standard linear
 model in the population:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{i} & = & x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}\\
E(u_{i}|x_{i}) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 with first-element unity.
 Under assumptions (1) and (2), if we have random draws 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

 from the population, then OLS is consistent and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-asymptotically normal for the parameters of interest, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 The problem we study in this chapter occurs when we observe only a censored
 version of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
19.2.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Binary Censoring
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We first cover the case where the censoring of the underlying response variable
 is extreme.
 Along with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

, we observe the binary response
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
w_{i}=1\left[y_{i}>r_{i}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we assume, for now, that the chance that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 equals 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What is the most natural way to proceed to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

? If we impose some strong assumptions on the underlying population and
 the nature of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

, then we can proceed with MLE.
 In particular, assume
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{i}|x_{i},r_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma^{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Assumption (4) implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}$
\end_inset

 actually satisfies the classical linear model (CLM).
 It also requires that 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, that is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
D(y_{i}|x_{i},r_{i})=D(y_{i}|x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Assumption (5) is satisfied if 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is randomized or if 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is chosen as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, or some combination of these alternatives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given assumption (4)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P(w_{i}=1|x_{i},r_{i}) & = & P(y_{i}>r_{i}|x_{i},r_{i})\nonumber \\
 & = & P\left(\left.\frac{u_{i}}{\sigma}>\frac{(r_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)}{\sigma}\right|x_{i},r_{i}\right)\nonumber \\
 & = & 1-\Phi\left[\frac{(r_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)}{\sigma}\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & \Phi\left[\frac{(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta-r_{i})}{\sigma}\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We can see that the bianry response, which indicates whether unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is in favor of the project at cost 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

, follows a probit model with parameter vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-\nicefrac{1}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, all the parameters, including 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

, are identified provided 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not perfectly collinear and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 varies across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in a way not perfectly linearly related to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Given the data censoring, the MLEs are the asymptotically efficient estimators
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

.
 Because the underlying population model is linear, we are interested in
 the slopes, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The costs of the binary censoring scheme are potentially severe.
 If we could observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, specifying 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

 would suffice for consistent estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

; in fact, we could just specify a linear projection and use OLS.
 With censoring, we must add more, and assumption (4) implies that the underlyin
g model satisfies the CLM.
 It is in this setting where discussion of the delterious effects of nonnormalit
y and heteroskedasticity when using probit models make sense.
 With data censoring, we are interested only in the parameters in the underlying
 linear model.
 Consequently, it is now legitimate to be concerned about the effects on
 the parameter estimates of heteroskedasticity or nonnormality in the underlying
 linear model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is zero for some subset of the population, a sensible population model
 is the Type I Tobit:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=\max(0,x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

under assumption (4).
 If we had a random sample, we would use Type I Tobit MLE to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 (naturally, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 would not come into play), and we would use the MLEs to estimate the means,
 say, from a Type I Tobit.
 Interestingly, if we have binary censoring, the estimation procedure is
 identical to that outlined for a linear model for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, provided that the 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 are all strictly positive.
 Because we do not observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, we cannot distinguish between equations (1) and (7) when 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}>0$
\end_inset

.
 But if we believe that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is zero for a nontrivial fraction of the population, any calculations should
 reflect that belief by using the Type I Tobit formulas for estimating partial
 effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 cannot take on negative values, the linear model we started with in equation
 (1) is not ideal.
 The following Tobit assumption
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y=\max(0,x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

leads to the same estimation method but implies a Tobit form for 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 If we think 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}>0$
\end_inset

 in the population, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=\exp(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is more attractive.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\log(y_{i})=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}$
\end_inset

, so that the previous analysis applies but with 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 replaced with 
\begin_inset Formula $\log(r_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 The differences in the functional forms for 
\begin_inset Formula $P(w_{i}=1|x_{i},r_{i})$
\end_inset

 provides a way to distinguish between the different assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
19.2.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Interval Coding
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now consider the linear model (1) in a scenario where the continuous,
 quantitative outcome, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, is only recorded to fall into a particular interval.
 In this case we say we have 
\series bold
interval-coded data
\series default
 (or 
\series bold
interval-censored data
\series default
).
 We are still interested in the population regress 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}<\cdots<r_{J}$
\end_inset

 denote the 
\emph on
known
\emph default
 interval limits; these are specified as part of the survey design.
 For example, rather than asking individuals to report actual annual income,
 they report the interval that their imcome falls into.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under the normality assumption in equation (4), we can estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 We can define
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
w_{i}=0 & \mbox{if}\ y_{i}\leq r_{1}\\
w_{i}=1 & \mbox{if}\ r_{1}<y_{i}\leq r_{2}\\
\vdots\\
w_{i}=J & \mbox{if}\ y>r_{J}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and easily obtain the conditional probabilities 
\begin_inset Formula $P(w_{i}=j|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $j=0,\ldots,J$
\end_inset

.
 The log likelihood for a random draw 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
l_{i}(\beta,\sigma) & = & 1\left[w_{i}=0\right]\log\left\{ \Phi\left[\frac{\left(r_{1}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta\right)}{\sigma}\right]\right\} +1\left[w_{i}=1\right]\log\left\{ \Phi\left[\frac{\left(r_{2}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta\right)}{\sigma}\right]-\Phi\left[\frac{\left(r_{1}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta\right)}{\sigma}\right]\right\} \nonumber \\
 &  & +\cdots+1\left[w_{i}=J\right]\log\left\{ 1-\Phi\left[\frac{\left(r_{J}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta\right)}{\sigma}\right]\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

The maximum likelihood estimators, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

, are often called 
\series bold
interval regression estimators
\series default
, with the understanding that the underlying population distribution is
 homoskedastic normal.
 We interpret the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 as if we had been able to run the regression 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots N$
\end_inset

.
 Imposing assumptions of the CLM allows us to estimate the parameters in
 the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

, even though the data are interval censored.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Sometimes the interval limits change across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, a possibility that causes no problems if we assume the limits are exogenous
 in the following sense:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
D(y_{i}|x_{i},r_{i1},\ldots,r_{iJ})=D(y_{i}|x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Generally, the limits can be a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 (because these are being conditioned on).
 The resulting log likelihood is exactly as in equation (11) with 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{j}$
\end_inset

 replaced with 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 Also, even if 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 is set to some value within the interval that contains 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, MLE estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 will be the same provided the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 determines the proper interval.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
19.2.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Censoring from Above and Below
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Two kinds of censoring are common: one is seen when the value of a variable
 is observed only when it is below a known cap, the other when it is above
 a known floor (or, in some cases, both).
 Consider first the case of 
\series bold
right censoring
\series default
 (censoring from above).
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, a censoring threshold, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

, is observed.
 When we randomly draw a unit from the population, we observe the explanatory
 variables, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 However, rather tahn observing the outcome on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, we effectively observe
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
w_{i}=\min(y_{i},r_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

If the underlying population distribution for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is continuous, the probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is zero, and so, if 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=r_{i}$
\end_inset

, we know that the observation is censored; if 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}<r_{i}$
\end_inset

, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=y_{i}$
\end_inset

; that is, we observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is (partially) discrete, there can be postivie probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=r_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 As a practical matter, when censoring points change across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, it is helpful in data sets to define a binary variable indicating whether
 an observation is censored.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For concreteness, we focus on the case where the population distribution
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is continuous.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{i}|x_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

 denote the conditional density.
 Under 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{i}|x_{i},r_{i})=D(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

, we can easily obtain the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},r_{i})$
\end_inset

 because, for 
\begin_inset Formula $w<r_{i}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(w_{i}\leq w|x_{i},r_{i})=P(y_{i}\leq w|x_{i})=F(w|x_{i};\theta)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot|x_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the cdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the probability density of 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},r_{i})$
\end_inset

 is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $f(w|x_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $w<r_{i}$
\end_inset

, that is for vlaues strictly less than the censoring point.
 Further,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(w_{i}=r_{i}|x_{i},r_{i})=P(y_{i}\geq r_{i}|x_{i},r_{i})=1-F(r_{i}|x_{i};\theta)
\]

\end_inset

It follows that we can write the probability density of 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},r_{i})$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g(w|x_{i},r_{i};\theta)=\left[f(w|x_{i};\theta)\right]^{1\left[w<r_{i}\right]}\left[1-F(r_{i}|x_{i};\theta)\right]^{1\left[w=r_{i}\right]}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The log-likelihood function for a random draw 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 (where we do not bother to distinguish between the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

true
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 value of theta and a generic value is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\log\left[g(w|x_{i},r_{i};\theta)\right]=1\left[w_{i}<r_{i}\right]\log\left[f(w_{i}|x_{i};\theta)\right]+1\left[w_{i}=r_{i}\right]\log\left[1-F(r_{i}|x_{i};\theta)\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and we sum this expression across all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 to obtain the log likelihood for the entire smaple.
 In the vast majority of cases, the conditions sufficient for MLE to be
 well behaved hold for sensored estimation because the model 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y|x;\theta)$
\end_inset

 is smooth 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An interesting feature of equation (15) is that we only need to observe
 the censoring point, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

, for censored observations.
 (However, we do need to know which observations are censored and which
 are not.) This feature of the MLE approach is useful because sometimes in
 applications to duration models, the censoring value is reported only for
 observations that are actually censored.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the leading case, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 follows a classical linear model in the population of interest, that is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
D(y_{i}|x_{i})=N(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta,\sigma^{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

in which case we have what is typically called the 
\series bold
censored normal regression model
\series default
.
 The log likelihood for the censored normal regression is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
l_{i}(\theta)=1\left[w_{i}<r_{i}\right]\log\left\{ \frac{1}{\sigma}\phi\left[\frac{\left(w_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta\right)}{\sigma}\right]\right\} +1\left[w_{i}=r_{i}\right]\log\left\{ 1-\Phi\left[\frac{(w_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)}{\sigma}\right]\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Often, a transformation, such as taking the natural 
\begin_inset Formula $\log$
\end_inset

, is needed to make equation (16) a reasonable assumption.
 In cases where no such transformation is available that ensures normality,
 the more general formulation in equation (15) can be used to obtain the
 log likelihood.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
19.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Overview of Sample Selection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now turn to estimation when a sample from subset of the population is
 used to estimate the unknown parameters.
 The term 
\series bold
selected sample
\series default
 is generally used to describe a sample that is not randomly drawn from
 the underlying population.
 There are a variety of 
\series bold
selections mechaism
\series default
 that result in selected samples.
 Some mechanisms are due to sample design, while others are due to the behavior
 of the units being sampled, inluding nonresponse on survery questions and
 attrition from social programs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An important general point to remember is that sample selection can only
 be an issue once the population of interest has been carefully specified.
 If we propose a model for a subset of a larger population, it is proper
 to proceed by obtaining a radom sample from that subpopulation and then
 using the standard econometric methods that we have covered thus far.
 That we do not have a random sample from the larger population does not
 affect our ability to conssitently estimate the parameters of the mmodel
 for the subpopulation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are a fewq common issues where nonrandom sampling is relevant and
 can cause serious problems.
 Somtimes data is missing on all variables for a subset of the population
 as a result of survey design.
 In other cases, units are randomly drawn from the population but data are
 missing on one or more variables for some units in the sample.
 Using a subset of a random sample because of missing data can lead to a
 sample selection problem.
 As we will see, if the reason the observations are missing is appropriately
 exogenous, using the subsample has no serious consequences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the next several sections, we cover a variety of sample selection issues,
 including tests and corrections.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
19.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When Can Sample Selection Be Ignored?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Here, we discuss under what circumstances does using a nonrandom sample
 from a specified population nevertheless consistently estimate the populaiton
 parameters?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
19.4.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Linear Models: Estimation by OLS and S2SLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We begin by obtaining conditions under which esitmation of the population
 model by two-stage least squares (2SLS) using a selected sample is consistent
 for the population parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We assume there is a population represented by the random vector 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y,z)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of explanatory variables, 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is the scalar response variable, and 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of instrumental variables.
 The population model is the standard single-equation linear model with
 possibly endogenous explanatory variables:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
y_{i}=\beta_{1}+\beta_{2}x_{i2}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{iK}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}\\
E(z_{i}u_{i})=0
\end{gather}

\end_inset

where we take 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}\equiv$
\end_inset

 for notational simplicity (an assumption that means 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 is almost certainly equal to unity, too).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Rather than obtaining a random sample from the population, we only use data
 points that satsify certain conditions.
 The idea is to think of drawing units randomly from the population, but
 now a random draw for unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i},z_{i})$
\end_inset

, is supplemented by drawing a 
\series bold
selection indicator
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 By definition, 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 if unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is used in the estimation, and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 if we do not use random draw 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, our 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

data
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 consist of 
\begin_inset Formula $\{(x_{i},y_{i},z_{i},s_{i}):i=1,\ldots,N\}$
\end_inset

, where the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 determines whether we observe all of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i},z_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because parameter identification should always be studied in a population,
 we let 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 denote a random variable with the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 In other words, 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y,z,s)$
\end_inset

 now represents the population.
 Therefore, to determine the properties of any estimation procedure using
 the selected sample, we need to know about the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and its dependence on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y,z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To obtian the conditions under which 2SLS on the selected sample consistently
 estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\{(x_{i},y_{i},z_{i},s_{i}):i=1,\ldots,N\}$
\end_inset

 is a random sample from the population.
 The 2SLS estimator using the selected sample can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\beta} & = & \left[\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{\prime}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\\
 &  & \times\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{\prime}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}y_{i}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{\beta} & = & \beta+\left[\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{\prime}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\nonumber \\
 &  & \times\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{\prime}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}z_{i}u_{i}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is easily seen from equation (20) that the key condition for consistency
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $E(s_{i}z_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, along with the rank condition on the selected sample.
 Formally, we have the following result
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
19.1 (Consistency of 2SLS under Sample Selection):
\series default
 
\emph on
In model (17), assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u^{2})<\infty$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{j}^{2})<\infty,\ j=1,\ldots,K$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{j}^{2})<\infty,\ j=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

.
 Further assume that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(s_{i}z_{i}u_{i}) & = & 0\\
\mbox{rank}E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}|s=1) & = & L\\
\mbox{rank}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}|s=1) & = & K
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Then, the 2SLS estimator using the selected sample is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-asymptotically normal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equation (20) essentially proves the consistency result under the assumptions
 of Theorem 19.1.
 Naturally, it is possible that the rank condition holds on the entire populatio
n but not the 
\begin_inset Formula $s=1$
\end_inset

 subset.
 Generally, one needs sufficient variation in both the explanatory variables
 and instruments in the subpopulation in order for the rank condition to
 hold.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is worth studying condtion (21) in some detail.
 First, it is not generally enough to assume condtion (19) holds.
 One case where condition (19) is sufficient occurs when 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $(z_{i},u_{i})$
\end_inset

, but this is a very strong assumption.
 In the leading case where 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}=x_{i}$
\end_inset

, so that estimation is by OLS, the condition is the same as independence
 between 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

, which is tantamount to assuming that some observations from an originial
 random sample are dropped randomly, without regard to the values of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 This has been called the 
\series bold
missing completely at random (MCAR)
\series default
 assumption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

More intersting are situations where selection can depend on exogenous variables
, but not on the unobserved error.
 An important sufficient conditions for assumption (21), easily verfied
 by applying iterated expectations, is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{i}|z_{i},s_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Assumption (24) allows selection to be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 but not with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, and has been called 
\series bold
exogenous sampling
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is easier to interpret this label by looking at a special case, whcih
 strengthens the sense in which the instruments are exogenous in the population.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|z_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a deterministic function of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, then assumption (24) holds.
 In other words, exogenous sampling occurs when 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}=h(z_{i})$
\end_inset

 for some nonrandom funciton 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\cdot)$
\end_inset

; that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a nonrandom funciton of exogenous variables.
 But we must remember that the sense in which the instruments are exogenous
 is given by the stronger asusmption 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|z_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the case with exogenous explanatory variables, condition (23) is equivalent
 to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{i}|x_{i},s_{i})=E(y_{i}|x_{i})=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the first equality is how we would define the exogenous sampling in
 the context of regression analysis.
 Notice that assumption (25) implies consistency of OLS on the selected
 sample regardless of whether data are missing on 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 or elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, or both.
 Assumption (25) rules out selection mechanisms that depend on the unobserved
 factors affecting 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 in equation (18).
 This assumption is related to (a conditional mean version of) the 
\series bold
missing at random (MAR)
\series default
 assumption.
 MAR, which is often stated in terms of conditional distributions, further
 assumes that the variables determing selection (
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 in this case) are always observed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A sufficient condition for assumption (24) is that conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 are independent, which is informatively written as 
\begin_inset Formula $D(s_{i}|z_{i},u_{i})=D(s_{i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

, or, because 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is binary
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(s_{i}=1|z_{i},u_{i})=P(s_{i}=1|z_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is observable, at least for part of the population, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is always unobservable, condition (26) is an example of what has been dubbed
 
\series bold
selection on observables
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Not surprisingly, the usual heteroskedasticity-robust variance matrix estimator,
 applied to the selected sample, is valid without further assumptions.
 If we add the homoskedasticity assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}^{2}|z_{i},s_{i})=E(u_{i}^{2})=\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 to the assumptions of Theorem 19.1, then we can show that the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

usual
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variance matrix estimator for 2SLS is asymptotically valid.
 Under the homoskedasticity assumption, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(s_{i}u_{i}^{2})=E(s_{i})\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(s_{i})$
\end_inset

 is just the franction of the subpopulation in the overall population.
 The estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 (without degrees-of-freedom adjustment) is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}\right)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}\hat{u}_{i}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{N}s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is simply the number of observations in the selected sample.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a funciton only of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, or if 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $(z_{i},u_{i})$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}^{2}|z_{i})=\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}^{2}|z_{i},s_{i})=\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous and we apply OLS on the selected sample, Theorem 19.1 implies
 that we can select the sample on the basis of the explanatory variables.
 Selection based on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 or on endogenous elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not allowed because then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|x_{i},s_{i})\neq E(u_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
19.4.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Nonlinear Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Results similar to those in the previous seciton hold for nonlinear models
 as well.
 We will cover explicitly the case of NLS and MLE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the NLS case, if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},s_{i})=E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

, so that selection is exogenous in the conditional mean sense, then NLS
 on the selected smaple is consistent.
 Sufficient is that 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a deterministic function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The consistency argument is simple: NLS on the selected sample solves
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \min_{\beta}}N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}\left[y_{i}-m(x_{i},\beta)\right]^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so it suffices to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{o}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})=m(x_{i},\beta_{o})$
\end_inset

 minimizes 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ s_{i}\left[y_{i}-m(x_{i},\beta)\right]^{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 By iterated expectations,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left\{ s_{i}\left[y_{i}-m(x_{i},\beta)\right]^{2}\right\} =E\left(s_{i}E\left\{ \left[y_{i}-m(x_{i},\beta)\right]^{2}|x_{i},s_{i}\right\} \right)
\]

\end_inset

Next, write 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[y_{i}-m(x_{i},\beta)\right]^{2}=u_{i}^{2}+2\left[m(x_{i},\beta_{o})-m(x_{i},\beta)\right]u_{i}+\left[m(x_{i},\beta_{o})-m(x_{i},\beta)\right]^{2}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}=y_{i}-m(x_{i},\beta_{o})$
\end_inset

.
 Bu assumption, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|x_{i},s_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left\{ \left[y_{i}-m(x_{i},\beta)\right]^{2}|x_{i},s_{i}\right\} =E(u_{i}^{2}|x_{i},s_{i})+\left[m(x_{i},\beta_{o})-m(x_{i},\beta)\right]^{2}
\]

\end_inset

and the second term is clearly minimized at 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=\beta_{o}$
\end_inset

.
 We do have to assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\emph on
unique
\emph default
 value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 that makes 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ s_{i}\left[m(x_{i},\beta)-m(x_{i},\beta_{o})\right]^{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

 zero.
 This is the identification condition on the subpopulation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It can be shown that, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y_{i}|x_{i},s_{i})=\mbox{Var}(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y_{i}|x_{i})=\sigma_{o}^{2}$
\end_inset

, then the usual, nonrobust NLS statistics are valid.
 If heteroskedasticity exists either in the population or the subpopulation,
 standard heteroskedasticity-robust inference can be used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another important case is the general CML setup.
 Assume that the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the same as the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
D(y_{i}|x_{i},s_{i})=D(y_{i}|x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Assumption (29) ensures that the MLE on the selected sample is consistent
 and the usual MLE statistics are valid.
 It is a stronger form of ignorability of selection, but it always holds
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a nonrandom function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, or if 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 CMLE on the selected sample solves
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{\theta}}N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}s_{i}l(y_{i},x_{i};\theta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $l(y_{i},x_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the log likelihood for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Now for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 maximizes 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[l(y_{i},x_{i};\theta)|x_{i}\right]$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 But 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[s_{i}l(y_{i},x_{i};\theta)\right]=E\left\{ s_{i}E\left[l(y_{i},x_{i};\theta)|x_{i},s_{i}\right]\right\} =E\left\{ s_{i}E\left[l(y_{i},x_{i};\theta)|x_{i}\right]\right\} $
\end_inset

 since, by assumption, the conditional distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},s_{i})$
\end_inset

 does not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[l(y_{i},x_{i};\theta)|x_{i}\right]$
\end_inset

 is maximized at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

, so is 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ s_{i}E\left[l(y_{i},x_{i};\theta)|x_{i}\right]\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 We must make the stronger assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{o}$
\end_inset

 is the unique maximum, just as in the previous cases.
 Inference can be carried out using the usual MLE statistics obtained from
 the selected subsample because the information equality now holds conditional
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 under assumption (29).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
19.5
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Selection on the Basis of the Response Variable: Truncated Regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

 denote a random draw from a population.
 In this section, we explicitly treat the case where the sample is selected
 on the basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In applying the following methods it is important to remember that there
 is an underlying population of interest, often described by a linear conditiona
l expectation: 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

.
 If we could observe a random sample from the population, then we would
 just use standard regression analysis.
 The problem comes about because the sample we can observe is chosen at
 least partly based on the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Unlike in the case where selection is based only on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, selection based on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 causes problems for standard OLS analysis on the selected sample.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a continuous random variable and that the selection rule takes the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{i}=1\left[a_{1}<y_{i}<a_{2}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}$
\end_inset

 are known constant such that 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}<a_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 A good way to think of the sample selection in this case is that we draw
 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

 randomly from the population.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 falls in the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{1},a_{2})$
\end_inset

, then we observe both 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is outside this interval, then we do not observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, all we know is that there is some subset of the population that does
 not enter our data set because of the selection rule.
 We know how to characterize the part of the population not being sampled
 because we know the constants 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In most applications we are still interested in estimation 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

.
 However, because of sample selection based on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, we must specify a full conditional distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Parameterize the conditional density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 are the conditional mean parameters and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $G\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of additional parameters.
 the cdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What we can use in estimation is the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
and
\emph default
 the fact that we observe 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i},x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 In other words, we must condition on 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}<y_{1}<a_{2}$
\end_inset

 or, equivalently, 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}=1$
\end_inset

.
 The cdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},s_{i}=1)$
\end_inset

 is simply
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{i}\leq\mathscr{Y})|x_{i},s_{i}=1)=\frac{P(y_{i}\leq\mathscr{Y},s_{i}=1|x_{i})}{P(s_{i}=1|x_{i})}
\]

\end_inset

Because 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is continuously distributed, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(s_{i}=1|x_{i})=P(a_{1}<y_{i}<a_{2}|x_{i})=F(a_{2}|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)-F(a_{1}|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)>0$
\end_inset

 for all possible values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The case 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}=\infty$
\end_inset

 corresponds to truncation only from below, in which case 
\begin_inset Formula $F(a_{2}|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)\equiv1$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}=-\infty$
\end_inset

 (truncation from above), then 
\begin_inset Formula $F(a_{1}|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)=0$
\end_inset

.
 To obtain the numerator when 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}<\mathscr{Y}<a_{2}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{i}\leq\mathscr{Y},s_{i}=1|x_{i})=P(a_{1}<y_{i}\leq\mathscr{Y}|x_{i})=F(\mathscr{Y}|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)-F(a_{1}|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)
\]

\end_inset

When we put this equation over 
\begin_inset Formula $P(s_{i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 and take the derivative with respect to the dummy argument 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{Y}$
\end_inset

, we obtain the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},s_{i}=1)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p(\mathscr{Y}|x_{i},s_{i}=1)=\frac{f(\mathscr{Y}|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)}{F(a_{2}|x_{1};\beta,\gamma)-F(a_{1}|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}<\mathscr{Y}<a_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given a model for 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{i}|x_{i};\beta,\gamma)$
\end_inset

, the log-likelihood function for any 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{i})$
\end_inset

 in the sample can be obtained by plugging 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 into equation (31) and taking the log.
 The CMLEs of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 using the selected sample are efficient in the class of estimators that
 do not use information about the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Standard errors and test statistics can be computed using the general theory
 of CMLE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In most applications of truncated samples, the population conditional distributi
on is assumed to be 
\begin_inset Formula $N(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

 in whcih case we have the 
\series bold
truncated Tobit model
\series default
 or 
\series bold
truncated normal regression model
\series default
.
 The truncated Tobit model is related to the censored Tobit model for data-censo
ring applications, but there is a key difference: in censored regression,
 we have a random sample of units and we observe covariates 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 for 
\emph on
all
\emph default
 people, even those for who the response is not known.
 If we drop observations entirely when the response is not observed, we
 obtain the truncated regression model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is not surprising that heteroskedasticity or nonnormality in truncated
 regression results in inconsistent estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 This outcome is unfortunate because, if not for the sample seletion problem,
 we could consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

, without specifying 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 or the conditional distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
19.6
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Incidental Truncation: A Probit Selection Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now turn to sample selection corrections when selection is determined
 by a probit model.
 This setup applies to problems different from those in Section 19.5, where
 the problem was that a survey or program was designed to intentionally
 exclude part of the population.
 We are now interested in selection problems that are due to incidental
 truncation, attrition in the context of program evaluation, and general
 nonresponse that leads to missing data on the response variable or the
 expalanatory variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
19.6.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Exogenous Explanatory Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This case differs in an important respect from top coding, where the censoring
 rule is known for each unit in the populaiton.
 With incidental truncation, we do know know the latent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, so we cannot use the response variable that we do know in a censored regressio
n analysis.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 were observed and exogenous and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

 were always observed, then we sould be in censored regression framework
 with censoring form below.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 were observed and exogenous but 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

 were observed only when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is, we would be in he truncated Tobit framework.
 But 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is allowed to depend on unobservalbes, and so we need a new framework.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can write the model for a random draw from the population as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{1i} & = & x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+u_{i}\\
y_{2i} & = & 1\left[x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}+v_{i}>0\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We discuss estimation of this model under the following set of assumptions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
19.1.
 
\series default
\emph default
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

 are always observed, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is observed only when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=1$
\end_inset

; (b) 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 are independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 with zero mean; (c) 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

; and (d) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|v_{i})=\gamma v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assumption 19.1a empahsizes the sample seleciton nature of the problem.
 Part b is a strong, but standard form of exogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We will see that assumption 19.1c is needed to derive a conditional expectiation
 given the selected sample.
 It is probably the most restrictive assumption because it is an explicit
 distributional assumption.
 Assuming 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(v_{i})=1$
\end_inset

 is without loss of generality because 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assumption 19.1d requires linearity in the population regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 It always holds if 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is bivariate normal, a standard assumption in these contexts, but assumption
 19.1d holds under weaker assumptions.
 In particular, we do not need to assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 itself is normally distributed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We call equations (32) and (33) the 
\series bold
Type II Tobit model
\series default
.
 This name is fine as a label, but we must understand that it is a model
 of sample selection, and it has nothing to do with 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 being a corner solution outcome.
 Unfortunately, in almost all treatments of this model, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is set to zero when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Setting 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 to zero (or any value) when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=0$
\end_inset

 is misleading and can lead to inappropriate use of the model.
 We also have some interest in the parameters of the selection equation
 (33).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because of the similarities in the statistical structures for the Type II
 Tobit model for sample selection and the exponential Type II Tobit model
 for corner solutions, now is a good time to review the differences in how
 one applies theses models.
 Recall that when using the ET2T for a corner solution response, the goal
 is to model the distribution of the 
\emph on
fully
\emph default
 observalbe outcome in a flexible way.
 The possible outomces include zero, which is a legitimate value and means
 we need to model the proability of a response at zero, as well as the density
 ofr positive vlaues.
 One can debate about which model is best but the important point is that
 there is no missing data problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The present situation is different from having a corner at zero.
 In the sampel selection setting, the variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 in equation (32), which often is in logarithmic form (in which case the
 stup appears wuire similar to the ET2T model), is not always observed.
 We are interested in the mean response in the populations, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1i}|x_{1i})$
\end_inset

, which is assume to follow a standard linear model.
 Consequently, once the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 have been consistently esitmated, it is trivial to interpret the esitmated
 equation because the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{1}$
\end_inset

 directly measure the partial effects of interest.
 In other words, in the sample selection context the parameter estimates
 are directly interpretable as partial effects on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1i}|x_{1i})$
\end_inset

, unlike in the ET2T case, where the various conditional means of the observed
 response have no simple forms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can allow a little more generality in the model by replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 in equation (33) with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2i}$
\end_inset

; then, as will become clear, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

 would only need to be observed whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is, whereas 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2i}$
\end_inset

 must always be observed.
 Because the selection equation is not typically a structural equation,
 it is undesirable to impose exclusion restricitions in equation (33).
 If a variable affecting 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is observed only along with 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

, the instrumental variables method that we cover in Seciton 19.6.2 is more
 attractive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To derive an estimating equation, let 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{1i},y_{2i},x_{i},u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 denote a random draw from the population.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is observed only when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=1$
\end_inset

 , what we can hope to estimate is 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i}=1)$
\end_inset

 [along with 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{2i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

].
 How does 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i}=1)$
\end_inset

 depend on the vector of interest, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

? First, under assumption 19.1 and equation (32),
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{1i}|x_{i},v_{i})=x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+E(u_{i}|x_{i},v_{i})=x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+E(u_{i}|v_{i})=x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+\gamma v_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the second equality follows because 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Equation (34) is very useful.
 The first thing to note is that, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=0$
\end_inset

 which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1i}|x_{i},v_{i})=E(y_{1i}|x_{i})=E(y_{1i}|x_{1i})=x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

, it follows immediately that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i})=E(y_{1i}|x_{1i})$
\end_inset

.
 In other words, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=0$
\end_inset

, then there is no sample selection problem, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 can be consistently estimated by OLS using the selected sample.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What if 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\ne0$
\end_inset

? Using iterated expectations on equation (34),
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i})=x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+\gamma E(v_{i}|x_{i},y_{2i})=x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+\gamma h(x_{i},y_{2i})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x_{i},y_{2i})=E(v_{i}|x_{i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

.
 If we knew 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x_{i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

, then, from Theorem 19.1, we could estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 from the regresison 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x_{i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

, using only the selected sample.
 Because the selected sample has 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=1$
\end_inset

, we need only find 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x_{i},1)$
\end_inset

.
 But 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x_{i},1)=E(v_{i}|v_{i}>-x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})=\lambda(x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\equiv\nicefrac{\phi(\cdot)}{\Phi(\cdot)}$
\end_inset

 is the inverse Mills ratio, and so we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i}=1)=x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+\gamma\lambda(x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Equation (35), which can be found in numerous places, makes it clear that
 an OLS regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

 using the selected sample omits the term 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda(x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})$
\end_inset

 and generally leads to inconsistent estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 The presence of selection bias can be viewed as an omitted variable problem
 in the selected sample.
 An interesting point is that, even though only 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

 appears in the population expectiation, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

, other elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 appear in the expectation on the subpopulation, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i}=1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equation (35) also suggests a way to consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 We can consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 using the selected sample by regression 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda(x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 The problem is that 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

 is unknown, so we cannot compute the additional regressor 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda(x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Nevertheless, a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

 is available from the first-stage probit estimation of the selected equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm*

\series bold
\shape up
19.1:
\series default
 
\emph on
(a) Obtain the probit esitmate 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\delta}_{2}$
\end_inset

 from the model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{2i}=1|x_{i})=\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

using all 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 observations.
 Then obtain the estimated inverse Mills rations 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\lambda}_{2i}\equiv\lambda(x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\delta}_{2})$
\end_inset

 (at least for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N_{1}$
\end_inset

).
 (b) Obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\gamma}$
\end_inset

 from the OLS regression on the selected sample,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{1i}\ \mbox{on}\ x_{1i},\hat{\lambda}_{2i},\ i=1,2,\ldots,N_{1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

These estimators are consistent and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

- asymptotically normal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The procedure is sometimes called 
\series bold
Heckit
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A very simple test for selection bias is available from regression (37).
 Under the null of no selection bias, 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\gamma=0$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i}=1)=\mbox{Var}(y_{1i}|x_{i})=\mbox{Var}(u_{i})$
\end_inset

, and so no homoskedasticity holds under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Further, the asymptotic variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\gamma}$
\end_inset

 (and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{1}$
\end_inset

) is not affected by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\delta}_{2}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, a standard 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 test on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\gamma}$
\end_inset

 is a valid test of the null hypothesis of no selection bias.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\neq0$
\end_inset

, obtaining a consistent estimate for the asymptotic variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{1}$
\end_inset

 is complicated for two reasons.
 The first is that, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\neq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i}=1)$
\end_inset

 is not constant.
 As we know, heteroskedasticity itself is easy to correct for using the
 robust standard errors.
 However, we should also acocunt for the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\delta}_{2}$
\end_inset

 is an estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The adjustment ot the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{\beta}_{1},\hat{\gamma})$
\end_inset

 because of the two-step estimation is cumbersome.
 It is 
\emph on
not 
\emph default
enough to simply make the standard errors heteroskedasticity-robust.
 Some statistical packages now have this feature built in.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As a technical point, we do not need 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

 to be a strict subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 to be identified, and procedure 19.1 doe carry through when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}=x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 However, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\delta}_{2}$
\end_inset

 does not have much variation in the sample, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\lambda}_{2i}$
\end_inset

 can be approximated well by a linear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=x_{1i}$
\end_inset

, this correlation can introduce sever collinearity among the regressors
 in (37) which can lead to large standard errors of the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}=x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 is identified only due to the nonlinearity of the inverse Mills ratio.
 Here, we still have identification for any value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 in equation (32), but it is unlikely we can estiamte 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 with much precision.
 Even if we can, we would have to wonder whether a statistically significant
 inverse Mills ratio term is due to sample selection or functional form
 misspecification in the populaiton model (32).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we replaced parts c and d in assumption 19.1 with the stronger assumption
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{1},v_{2})$
\end_inset

 is bivariate normal with mean zero, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{i})=\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(u_{i},v_{i})=\sigma_{uv}^{2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(v_{i})=1$
\end_inset

, then partial maximum likelihood estimation can be used.
 Partial MLE will be more efficient than the two-step procudre under joint
 normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, and it will produce standard errors and likelihood ratio statistics that
 can be used directily.
 The drawbacks are that it is less robust than the two-step procedure and
 that it is sometimes difficult to get the problem to converge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The reason we cannot perform full conditional MLE is that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is only observed when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, while we can use the full density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, which is 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{2i}|x_{i})=\left[\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})\right]^{y_{i2i}}\left[1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})\right]^{1-y_{2i}},\ y_{2i}=0,1$
\end_inset

, we can only use the density 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{1i}|y_{2i},x_{i})$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=1$
\end_inset

.
 To find 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{1i}|y_{2i},x_{i})$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=1$
\end_inset

, we can use Bayes' rule to write 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{1i}|y_{2i},x_{i})=\nicefrac{f(y_{2i}|y_{1i},x_{i})f(y_{1i}|x_{i})}{f(y_{2i}|x_{i})}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{1i}|y_{2i}=1,x_{i})=\nicefrac{P(y_{2i}=1|y_{1i},x_{i})f(y_{1i}|x_{i})}{P(y_{2i}=1|x_{i})}$
\end_inset

.
 But, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}|x_{i}\sim N(x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1},\sigma_{u}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Further, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=1\left[x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}+\sigma_{uv}\sigma_{u}^{-2}(y_{1i}-x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta)+e_{i}>0\right]$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{1i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\sim N(0,1-\sigma_{uv}^{2}\sigma_{u}^{-2})$
\end_inset

 (this conclusion follows from standard conitional distribution results
 for joint normal random variables).
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{2i}=1|y_{1i},x_{i})=\Phi\left\{ \left[x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}+\sigma_{uv}\sigma_{u}^{-2}(y_{1i}-x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]\left(1-\sigma_{uv}^{2}\sigma_{u}^{-2}\right)^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Combining all of these pieces [and noting the cancellation of 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{2i}=1|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
l_{i}(\theta) & = & (1-y_{i2})\log[1-\Phi(x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})]+y_{2i}\left(\log\Phi\left\{ \left[x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}+\sigma_{uv}\sigma_{u}^{-2}(y_{1i}-x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta)\right]\right.\right.\\
 &  & \left.\left.\times\left(1-\sigma_{uv}^{2}\sigma_{u}^{-2}\right)^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\right\} +\log\phi\left[\nicefrac{\left(y_{1i}-x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}\right)}{\sigma_{u}}\right]-\log(\sigma_{u})\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The partial log likelihood is obtained by summing 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 across all observations; 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=1$
\end_inset

 picks out when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is observed and therefore contains information for estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
19.7
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Indental Truncation: A Tobit Selection Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now study the case where more information is available on sample selection
 primarily in the context of incidental truncation.
 In particular, we assume that selection is based on the otucome of a Tobit,
 rather than a probit, equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
19.7.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Exogenous Explanatory Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now consider the case where the selection equation is of the censored
 Tobit form.
 The population model is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{1i} & = & x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+u_{i}\\
y_{2i} & = & \max\left(0,x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}+v_{i}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

 is always observed in the population by 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is observed only when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}>0$
\end_inset

.
 A standard example occurs when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is the log of the hourly wage offer and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is weekly or annual hours of labor supply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
19.3:
\series default
 
\emph on
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

 is always observed in the population, but 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is observed only when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}>0$
\end_inset

; (b) 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

; (c) 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{v}^{2})$
\end_inset

; and (d) 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|v_{i})=\gamma v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These assumptions are very similar to the assumptions for a probit selection
 equation.
 The only difference is that 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 now has an unknown variance, since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is a censored as opposed to a binary variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We call equations (38) and (39) the 
\series bold
Type III Tobit model
\series default
, but we emphasize that equation (38) is the structural population equation
 of interest and that equation (39) simply determines when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is observed.
 As with a probit selection equation, it makes no sense to define 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 to be , say, zero, just because we do not observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The starting point is equation (34), just as in the probit selection case.
 Now, define the selection indicator as 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{2i}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}>0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{2i}=0$
\end_inset

 otherwise.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, it follows immediately that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(y_{1i}|x_{i},v_{i},s_{2i})=x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+\gamma v_{i}
\]

\end_inset

This equation means that, if we could observe 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, then an OLS regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 using the selected subsample would consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta_{1},\gamma)$
\end_inset

.
 While 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 cannot be observed when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=0$
\end_inset

 (because when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=0$
\end_inset

, we only know that 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\leq-x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2})$
\end_inset

, for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=y_{2i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, if we knew 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

, we would know 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}>0$
\end_inset

.
 It seems reasonable that, because 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

 can be consistently estimated by Tobit on the whole sample, we cna replace
 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 with consistent estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm*

\series bold
\shape up
19.3:
\series default
 
\emph on
(a) Estimate equation (39) by standard Tobit using all 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 observations.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}>0$
\end_inset

 (say 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,N_{1}$
\end_inset

), define
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{v}_{i}=y_{2i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\delta}_{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

(b) Using observations for which 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}>0$
\end_inset

, estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

, by the OLS regression
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{1i}\ \mbox{on}\ x_{1i},\hat{v}_{i},\ i=1,\ldots,N_{1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This regression produces consistent, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

- asymptotically normal estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 under assumption 19.3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The statistic to test for selectivity bias is just the usual 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistic on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

 in regression (41).
 It seems likely that there is an efficiency over procedure 19.1.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 were known we could use regression (41) for the entire population, there
 would definitely be an efficiency gain: the error variance is reduced by
 conditioning on 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

along with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, and there would be no heteroskedasticity in the population.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Unlike in the probit selection case, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}=x_{i}$
\end_inset

 causes no problems here: 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 always has separate variance from 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

 because of variation in 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

.
 We do not need to rely on the nonlinearity of the inverse Mills ratio.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For partial maximum likelihood estimation, we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is jointly normal, and we use the density for 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{2i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 for the entire sample and the conditional density 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i},s_{2i}=1)=f(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

 for the selected sample.
 This approach is fairly straight forward because, when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i}\sim N\left[x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+\gamma(y_{2i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}),\eta^{2}\right]$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}=\sigma_{u}^{2}-\nicefrac{\sigma_{uv}^{2}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}$
\end_inset

.
 The log likelihood for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
l_{i}(\theta)=s_{2i}\log f(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i};\theta)+\log f(y_{2i}|x_{i};\delta_{2},\sigma_{v}^{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{1i}|x_{i},y_{2i};\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left[x_{1i}^{\prime}\beta_{1}+\gamma(y_{2i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}),\eta^{2}\right]$
\end_inset

 distribution, evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{2i}|x_{i};\delta_{2},\sigma_{v}^{2})$
\end_inset

 is the standard censored Tobit density.
 The usual MLE theory can be used even though the log-likelihood funciton
 is not based on a full conditional density.
\end_layout

